  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe key insight is to use a multi-resolution optimization strategy that leverages both global and local search methods. We start with a coarse grid (n=188) using Differential Evolution for global exploration, then refine the solution with L-BFGS-B for local optimization. This two-phase approach helps escape local minima while ensuring we find a high-quality solution. The bottleneck addressed is the limited exploration capability of local search methods alone, which often get stuck in suboptimal regions. By combining global and local search, we increase the chances of finding the global minimum.\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe minimize the overlap integral C_5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx over h:[0,2]\u2192[0,1] with \u222bh(x)dx=1. The discrete version is:\nC_5 = max_k (sum_i h[i] * (1-h[i+k])) * (2.0/n_points)\nConstraints:\n0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\nsum(h[i]) = n_points/2\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   - If parent solution (n=188) is available, use it as initial guess\n   - Generate 9 additional random initial guesses\n   - Each guess is scaled to satisfy sum(h)=n_points/2\n\n2. Global optimization (Differential Evolution):\n   - Objective function: max_k (sum_i h[i]*(1-h[i+k])) * (2.0/n_points) + penalty for constraints\n   - Bounds: [0,1] for each h[i]\n   - Population size: 100\n   - Max evaluations: 10000\n   - Time limit: 500 seconds\n\n3. Local optimization (L-BFGS-B):\n   - Objective function: max_k (sum_i h[i]*(1-h[i+k])) * (2.0/n_points) + penalty for equality constraint\n   - Bounds: [0,1] for each h[i]\n   - Max iterations: 10000\n   - Time limit: 100 seconds\n\n4. Upsampling to n=376:\n   - If time allows, interpolate best solution to n=376\n   - Adjust integral to satisfy \u222bh(x)dx=1\n   - Repeat global/local optimization on finer grid\n\n5. Best solution tracking:\n   - Track best solution found across all resolutions\n   - Re-evaluate objective without penalty for final selection\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n- Global phase: 10000 evaluations with Differential Evolution\n- Local phase: 10000 iterations with L-BFGS-B\n- Penalty factors: 1e10 for global phase, 1e5 for local phase\n- Initial population: 10 points (parent + 9 random)\n- Time allocation: 500s global, 100s local, 500s for upsampling\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\n- DE: ~353 million operations for n=188 (0.5s in C but longer in Python)\n- L-BFGS-B: ~353 million operations for n=188 (0.1s in C but longer in Python)\n- Upsampling: O(n^2) for interpolation and integral adjustment\n- Fallback: Return best solution found if time expires\n- Repair mechanism: Clip values to [0,1] and adjust integral if needed\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Check 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 and sum(h)=n_points/2\n- Re-compute objective without penalty for final solution\n- Return format: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n- c5_bound = min_k (sum_i h[i]*(1-h[i+k])) * (2.0/n_points)\n\nThe solution leverages the parent solution as a starting point while exploring the solution space through global and local search. The two-phase optimization balances exploration and exploitation, with the global phase finding promising regions and the local phase refining the solution. Upsampling allows leveraging the coarse solution for finer details, potentially improving the bound.\n\nThe algorithm ensures constraint satisfaction through penalties and repairs, and tracks the best solution found. The objective is computed as the maximum overlap integral across all shifts, with the goal of minimizing this value.\n\nThe implementation uses:\n- Differential Evolution for global search\n- L-BFGS-B for local search\n- Linear interpolation for upsampling\n- Penalty functions for constraints\n- Time tracking to ensure within budget\n\nThe fallback solution is the best candidate found during the search, ensuring we always return a valid solution even if time expires.",
